00:16:16.000 --> 00:17:01.000
ready to give such dominance to this one needs that would override everything else. And this continuum goes all the way from, you know, supporting it attributinally to willing to contribute money, to participating in a minor role in terrorist organizations, all the way to the willingness to become a suicide bomber and sacrifice when lives on alter of the cause. It's a continuum. It's not a dichotomy. I mean, terrorism is a part of life in this 21st century. Do you have data to to show if the number of terrorists are increasing? No, I don't. But I think there are hundreds of terrorist groups operating.
准备好给予这种需求如此大的支配地位，以至于它会凌驾于一切之上。这个连续体一直延伸到，你知道，从归因上支持它，到愿意捐钱，到在恐怖组织中参与次要角色，一直到愿意成为自杀式炸弹袭击者并为事业牺牲生命。这是一个连续体。它不是二分法。我的意思是，恐怖主义是21世纪生活的一部分。你有数据表明恐怖分子的数量是否在增加吗？不，我没有。但我认为有数百个恐怖组织在运作。

27
00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:47.000
I think the last number was around 300 that I've seen. And what seems to be increasing is the white suprematist groups that can become violent. You know, there is a polarization of societies, both in Europe and the United States. and there's legitimations of xenophobic attitudes and populist ideologies. And this become now the mainstream, whereas before they were kind of despised fringe in society. You see it in Germany. The Alternative of Germany is now in the Bundestag. You see it in France.
我想我看到的最后一个数字是300左右。而且看起来在增加的是可能变得暴力的白人至上主义团体。你知道，欧洲和美国社会都存在两极分化，仇外态度和民粹主义意识形态正在合法化。这现在成了主流，而以前它们在社会中是被鄙视的边缘群体。你在德国看到了这一点。德国选择党现在在联邦议院。你在法国看到了这一点。

28
00:17:47.000 --> 00:18:22.000
You see it in Hungary. You see it in Poland. nationalism and populism are becoming more and more acceptable in society, and that in turn legitimizes the French groups that share the same ideas and are willing to go one step further through employment of violence. So I think the number of attacks have increased in the United States against synagogue, against mosques from in 2016-2016 onward. And you see the same tendencies the world over.
你在匈牙利看到了这一点。你在波兰看到了这一点。民族主义和民粹主义在社会中变得越来越被接受，这反过来又使那些持有相同想法并愿意通过使用暴力更进一步的边缘群体合法化。所以我认为，从2016年开始，美国针对犹太教堂、清真寺的袭击数量有所增加。你在世界各地也看到了同样的趋势。

29
00:18:22.000 --> 00:19:22.000
Of course, not to speak about Islamic extremism that has been rampant in the last, you know, several decades and is not going away anytime soon. That's really one of the most pressing issues facing national security and international security today. It is indeed. It is indeed. And the danger is clear and present and of great proportions. I know that, you know, very esteemed colleague, Stephen Pinker, has been showing how the degree of violence have been declining over the centuries and including in the last century. But, you know, this is a kind of inductivist argument, the idea that because it has been decreasing, we can expect further decreases. And I do not think that this is the case. I think things can be reversed, and especially with the great technology, nuclear technology,
当然，更不用说过去几十年来猖獗且短期内不会消失的伊斯兰极端主义。这确实是当今国家安全和国际安全面临的最紧迫问题之一。确实如此。确实如此。危险是清晰可见的，而且规模巨大。我知道，非常受人尊敬的同事斯蒂芬·平克（Stephen Pinker）一直在展示暴力程度在几个世纪以来是如何下降的，包括在上个世纪。但是，这是一种归纳论证，即因为暴力一直在减少，我们就可以期待进一步减少。我不认为情况是这样。我认为事情是可以逆转的，特别是有了伟大的技术，核技术，

30
00:19:22.000 --> 00:20:08.000
all kinds of weapons of mass destruction that could fall into the hands of fanatics. The danger is great, and society needs to mobilize itself to fight against it with all means at our disposal. Do you think there is enough international call to do so? I do not think so. I think especially in our country, there's been very little attention to the problem in its general form. We have been assuming that to fight terrorism, the only way is to employ them military and the police and do swore plots and so forth. But the problem is social psychological. You cannot kill an ideology.
各种可能落入狂热分子手中的大规模杀伤性武器。危险很大，社会需要动员起来，用我们所能使用的一切手段与之斗争。你认为是否有足够的国际呼吁来这样做吗？我不这么认为。我认为特别是在我们国家，对这种一般形式的问题关注很少。我们一直假设打击恐怖主义的唯一方法是动用军队和警察，粉碎阴谋等等。但这个问题是社会心理学的。你无法杀死一种意识形态。

31
00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:36.000
Governor Romney quipped some years ago that we... We cannot kill our way out of this mess. Indeed we cannot. The military means are necessary but insufficient. You have to understand what prompts individuals to embark on violence and the embracement of radical ideologies. You have to prevent it. It has to be a whole society effort.
罗姆尼州长几年前打趣说，我们……我们无法通过杀戮来摆脱这种混乱。确实我们不能。军事手段是必要的，但还不够。你必须了解是什么促使个人走上暴力之路并拥抱激进意识形态。你必须预防它。这必须是全社会的努力。

32
00:20:36.000 --> 00:21:21.000
It starts with the treatment of immigrants and refugees. It starts with respect. of alternative religions. It has to involve trusting the police, police community relations, community institutions, religious institutions, social services, the educational institutions. It has to be a whole society effort. It cannot be left to the military because the problem is not only resolvable on the military grounds. And you're often interviewed in the media about the topic of de-radicalization, and two recent stories were about, you were a part of, the stories highlighted recently reformed white supremacists.
它始于对移民和难民的对待。它始于对其他宗教的尊重。它必须涉及信任警察、警民关系、社区机构、宗教机构、社会服务、教育机构。这必须是全社会的努力。它不能留给军队，因为问题不仅仅可以在军事基础上解决。你经常在媒体上接受关于去激进化主题的采访，最近有两个故事是你参与的，故事重点介绍了最近改过自新的白人至上主义者。

33
00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:55.000
Can you explain the process of how a person can become de-radicalized? There are many different forms of the radicalization. Basically, the radicalization is the reverse of radicalization. It involves the same three elements of need, narrative, and network. that I mentioned before. For example, in our work on the German neo-Nazis, those who left the movement, who became deradicalized, either had their alternative needs evoked.
你能解释一下一个人如何去激进化的过程吗？去激进化有许多不同的形式。基本上，去激进化是激进化的逆过程。它涉及我之前提到的需求、叙事和网络这三个要素。例如，在我们对德国新纳粹分子的研究中，那些离开该运动、变得去激进化的人，要么是他们的替代需求被唤起。

34
00:21:55.000 --> 00:22:26.000
They became older, they wanted to get married, they wanted to have a profession. They got tired of just putting all their eggs in one basket. So that's the need component. some individuals became disenchanted with the ideology. They saw that ideology is just a kind of tokenism, that people are not serious about it, that the people in the movement are bent on partying
他们变老了，他们想结婚，他们想拥有一份职业。他们厌倦了把所有鸡蛋放在一个篮子里。所以这是需求部分。有些人对意识形态感到幻灭。他们看到意识形态只是一种象征主义，人们并不认真对待它，运动中的人一心只想聚会，

35
00:22:26.000 --> 00:23:11.000
and getting into fights and brutality as opposed to really caring about the societal change that they were purported to care about. Some people were disenchanted with the network. They felt that this band of brothers that was promised them that gave them permission to become violent isn't really a band of brothers. There's a lot of knifing in the back, a lot of betrayal, lack of camaraderie. So, you know, all these are elements that can promote the radicalization. We have also seen the radicalization that is spontaneous.
参与打架和残暴行为，而不是真正关心他们声称关心的社会变革。有些人对网络感到幻灭。他们觉得承诺给他们的“兄弟连”——那个允许他们变得暴力的群体——并不是真正的兄弟连。背后捅刀子的情况很多，背叛很多，缺乏同志情谊。所以，所有这些都是可以促进去激进化的要素。我们也看到了自发的去激进化。

36
00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:47.000
For example, in Egypt and Algeria, there were radical movements that deradicalized, as it were spontaneously. But it was not quite spontaneous. It happened when it became clear that the violence would not promote significance. When the public turns against al-jihad in Egypt, the al-jihad. organization because they were killing tourists and tourism is the lifeblood of Egyptian economy. So the public turns against them.
例如，在埃及和阿尔及利亚，有一些激进运动自发地去激进化了。但这并不完全是自发的。当暴力显然无法促进重要性时，它就发生了。当公众反对埃及的“圣战”组织时，因为他们杀害游客，而旅游业是埃及经济的命脉。所以公众反对他们。

37
00:23:47.000 --> 00:24:35.000
Their weapons caches were confiscated, they were put in jail, and then they realized that the Koran prohibits violence. So, you know, once it is clear that violence is not going to serve the means of significance, they are open and their mind becomes open to. alternative ideas and they see that religion actually prohibits violence, something they did not see, strangely enough, when the going was strong. There are attempts to eradicate people in a systematic way, for example, in Saudi Arabia, in Sri Lanka, in detention camps where suspected and arrested terrorists are held.
他们的武器库被没收，他们被关进监狱，然后他们意识到《古兰经》禁止暴力。所以，一旦清楚暴力无法服务于重要性的手段，他们就开放了，他们的思想变得对替代思想开放，他们看到宗教实际上禁止暴力，奇怪的是，当他们势头强劲时，他们并没有看到这一点。有一些尝试以系统的方式去激进化人们，例如在沙特阿拉伯、斯里兰卡，在关押嫌疑和被捕恐怖分子的拘留营中。

38
00:24:35.000 --> 00:25:36.000
And these attempts bring together the terrorists with governmental clerics that espouse a different interpretation of their religion. They offer them alternative ways of gaining significance through vocational training, through finding jobs, ways of integrating into society. So all of these have to do with addressing the need in a different way. extricating the person from the network that was supportive of violence and inserting them in the network that is anti-violence, sometimes their families, and exposing them to narratives that interpret religion or provide alternative ideologies as to what significance, how significance can be attained in ways other than violence. What can communities and governments do to prevent terrorism?
这些尝试将恐怖分子与宣扬不同宗教解释的政府教士聚集在一起。他们通过职业培训、通过寻找工作、通过融入社会的方式，为他们提供获得重要性的替代途径。所以所有这些都与以不同方式解决需求有关。将个人从支持暴力的网络中解脱出来，并将其插入一个反暴力的网络中，有时是他们的家庭，并让他们接触那些解释宗教或提供关于如何以暴力以外的方式获得重要性的替代意识形态的叙事。社区和政府可以做些什么来预防恐怖主义？

